Java 8 introduced lambda expressions, which allow for concise functional programming. For example, you can use a lambda to sort a list of strings by their length:

```java
List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Alice", "Bob", "Charlie");
names.sort((s1, s2) -> Integer.compare(s1.length(), s2.length()));
System.out.println(names); // Output: [Bob, Alice, Charlie]
```

Here is an example of a Java 8 lambda expression. It sorts a list of strings alphabetically.

```java
List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Alice", "Bob", "Charlie");
names.sort((s1, s2) -> s1.compareTo(s2));
```

This lambda `(s1, s2) -> s1.compareTo(s2)` takes two strings and returns the result of their comparison, which is used by the `sort` method.
